# Step 3: Expenditure account, done properly

## Formula

[
GDP_{movie}^{(E)} = C + I + G + (X - M)
]

## 3A. Consumption (C)

### Tickets

For expenditure at market prices, use purchaser spend:

[
C_{tickets} = IndiaGross_{agg} = 2722.19
]

### Concessions

Assume theatre concessions and related in-cinema spend are:

[
r_{conc} = 27% \text{ of India gross}
]

[
C_{concessions} = 2722.19 \times 0.27 = 734.99
]

### Adjacent audience spend

Nearby food, parking, local transport, fan events, small merch impulse. Use:

[
r_{adj} = 4% \text{ of India gross}
]

[
C_{adjacent} = 2722.19 \times 0.04 = 108.89
]

So total consumption:

[
C = 2722.19 + 734.99 + 108.89 = 3566.07
]

---

## 3B. Investment (I)

Use all-in franchise cost:

[
I = 505.00
]

Break it:

[
I_{production} = 78% \times 505 = 393.90
]

[
I_{P&A} = 18% \times 505 = 90.90
]

[
I_{distribution} = 4% \times 505 = 20.20
]

Check:

[
393.90 + 90.90 + 20.20 = 505.00
]

---

## 3C. Government (G)

You were right. Zeroing this out invites a critique.

For a large domestic franchise shot across jurisdictions, even if public data is patchy, it is reasonable to assume some combination of:

* state filming concessions,
* location rebates,
* fee waivers,
* tourism-linked facilitation,
* administrative support.

Use a **conservative guesstimate**:

[
G = 1.5% \times I
]

[
G = 0.015 \times 505 = 7.58
]

So:

* **Government-linked support = ₹7.58 crore**

---

## 3D. Exports (X)

### Overseas theatrical

[
X_{overseas\ theatrical} = 660.31
]

### Foreign share of rights

Not all rights are export revenue. Some are domestic. Assume:

[
r_{foreign\ rights} = 20%
]

[
X_{foreign\ rights} = 380 \times 0.20 = 76.00
]

So:

[
X = 660.31 + 76.00 = 736.31
]

---

## 3E. Imports (M)

Again, this must be explicit.

Assume:

* imported production inputs = 8% of production spend
* imported marketing/distribution services = 10% of P&A + distribution spend

### Production imports

[
M_{prod} = 0.08 \times 393.90 = 31.51
]

### Marketing/distribution imports

[
M_{mkt/dist} = 0.10 \times (90.90 + 20.20) = 11.11
]

So total imports:

[
M = 31.51 + 11.11 = 42.62
]

---

## 3F. Final expenditure result

[
GDP_{movie}^{(E)} = 3566.07 + 505.00 + 7.58 + (736.31 - 42.62)
]

[
GDP_{movie}^{(E)} = 3566.07 + 505.00 + 7.58 + 693.69
]

[
GDP_{movie}^{(E)} = 4772.34
]

## Expenditure headline

[
\boxed{GDP_{movie}^{(E)} = ₹4,772.34 \text{ crore}}
]

That is your **direct GDP-style economic footprint**.

